<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Additional Related Work</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training dynamics of ICL</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Varre et al. (2025)</a></cite> shows that <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-grams are approximate stationary points in the training of two-layers transformers.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025a)</a></cite> studies the training dynamics of a one-layer linear transformer with linear attention on linear regression tasks.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sander et al. (2024)</a></cite> characterize the training dynamics of a one-linear layer transformer on auto-regressive tasks, showing how ICL emerges.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al. (2023a)</a></cite> show that for linear regression problems and a linear transformer, the global minimizer of the training loss corresponds to performing one step of preconditioned gradient descent.
In contrast, our approach focuses on the influence of the pre-training distribution on ICL. We therefore assume that the model is sufficiently expressive and trained optimally enough to approximate the Bayes optimal predictor. We refer to recent works on optimization dynamics of transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barboni et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azizian et al. (2025)</a></cite> and on the approximation capabilities of transformers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Approximation capabilities of transformers</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The foundational works of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al. (2023)</a></cite> demonstrate that transformers can implement gradient descent. This has led to a fruitful line of work studying the algorithmic capabilities of transformers.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2023)</a></cite> show that transformers can implement a wide variety of statistical methods.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025a)</a></cite> shows how transformers can implement functional gradient descent on categorical data, generalizing previous works.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite> shows how attention transformers can implement gradient descent on a ReLU network.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sander and Peyré (2025)</a></cite> explicitly constructs a transformer that implements kernel causal regression.
On a more abstract perspective,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Furuya et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kratsios and Furuya (2025)</a></cite> show that (causal) transformers can approximate any (causal) map between measures.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Weinan (2024)</a></cite> studies quantitatively the approximation properties of transformers on "sparse memory" target functions.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025a)</a></cite> obtains explicit approximation bounds for numerical ICL tasks.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Experimental Results</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Linear Regression</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide comprehensive experimental results for linear regression tasks (detailed in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Linear Regression ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) using Student-<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and generalized normal pretraining distributions.
This section presents the ICL error as a function of context length (ICL step) for Student-<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> priors with degrees of freedom <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\in\{3,5,10,\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\in\{3,5,10,\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> and generalized normal priors with shape parameters <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\{1,1.5,2,2.5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\{1,1.5,2,2.5\}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the experimental settings in <a href="#S4.F1" title="In 4 Experiments ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results in <a href="#A2.F6" title="In B.1 Linear Regression ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> clearly demonstrate the fundamental trade-off in selecting pretraining distributions for ICL: heavy-tailed priors (small <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>) achieve superior performance under distribution shift, while light-tailed priors (large <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>) excel on in-distribution tasks.
In contrast, <a href="#A2.F7" title="In B.1 Linear Regression ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows that varying the shape parameter of generalized normal priors produces more subtle effects on ICL performance in the linear regression setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also notice on <a href="#A2.F6" title="In B.1 Linear Regression ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ B.1 Linear Regression ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> that longer context lengths are mostly beneficial for in-distribution tasks: as the perturbation magnitude increases, the performance gains from longer contexts diminish. This is in line with <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Task selection ‣ 3 Theoretical framework ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>: the performance gain per new example is determined by the prior probability of the task, which decreases with larger perturbations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_400000.png" id="A2.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Student-t, <math id="A2.F6.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=3.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/x8.png" id="A2.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Student-t, <math id="A2.F6.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=5.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=5.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_500000.png" id="A2.F6.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Student-t, <math id="A2.F6.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=10.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=10.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/x9.png" id="A2.F6.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gaussian limit, <math id="A2.F6.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A2.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Linear regression with Student-<math id="A2.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> pretraining distributions: MSE as a function of ICL step for different task shift magnitudes. Heavy-tailed priors (<math id="A2.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=3</annotation></semantics></math>) show superior robustness to distribution shift, while light-tailed priors (<math id="A2.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, Gaussian) perform better on unperturbed tasks. The Ridge regression baseline provides a reference that remains constant across perturbation magnitudes.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/x10.png" id="A2.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GenNormal, <math id="A2.F7.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_250000.png" id="A2.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GenNormal, <math id="A2.F7.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.5</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F7.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/x11.png" id="A2.F7.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F7.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GenNormal, <math id="A2.F7.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F7.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/x12.png" id="A2.F7.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A2.F7.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GenNormal, <math id="A2.F7.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2.5</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A2.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Linear regression with generalized normal pretraining distributions: MSE as a function of ICL step for different task shift magnitudes. The shape parameter <math id="A2.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> has a more modest impact on performance compared to Student-<math id="A2.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> distributions, with all variants showing similar convergence patterns across perturbation levels.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We present an extended analysis of the generalization results from <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4 Experiments ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> in <a href="#A2.F8" title="In B.1 Linear Regression ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, examining how the number of pretraining tasks <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> affects performance across different Student-<math id="A2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> tail parameters <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>. These results validate <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3.3 Generalization error ‣ 3 Theoretical framework ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, showing that heavy-tailed priors require more training tasks to achieve comparable performance to light-tailed priors.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/x13.svg" id="A2.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><math id="A2.F8.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=100</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/x14.svg" id="A2.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><math id="A2.F8.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=500</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/x15.svg" id="A2.F8.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><math id="A2.F8.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1000</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F8.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/x16.svg" id="A2.F8.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><math id="A2.F8.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5000</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A2.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Generalization analysis for linear regression across different numbers of pretraining tasks <math id="A2.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> for a context length of 64. As predicted by <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3.3 Generalization error ‣ 3 Theoretical framework ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, heavy-tailed priors (small <math id="A2.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>) require more tasks to achieve performance comparable to light-tailed priors, but eventually outperform them under distribution shift. The crossover point shifts to larger <math id="A2.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> for heavier-tailed distributions.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we provide an ablation study on the effect of the variance. All other experiments are designed so that the pretraining distribution has unit variance in each dimension. In <a href="#A2.F9" title="In B.1 Linear Regression ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we vary the variance of a standard Gaussian pretraining distribution and observe it only changes the ICL performance for in-distribution tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/x17.svg" id="A2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A2.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation on the effect of variance for Gaussian pretraining distributions in linear regression. Only in-distribution performance is affected by the variance, with larger variances leading to worse performance.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Ornstein–Uhlenbeck Processes</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present detailed experimental results for Ornstein–Uhlenbeck (OU) stochastic processes (described in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Linear Stochastic Differential Equations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) using both Student-<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and generalized normal pretraining distributions.
The figures show ICL error as a function of context length for Student-<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> priors with degrees of freedom <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\in\{3,5,10,\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\in\{3,5,10,\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> (matching <a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.1 Linear Regression ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and generalized normal priors with shape parameters <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\{1,1.5,2,2.5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\{1,1.5,2,2.5\}</annotation></semantics></math> (matching <a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.1 Linear Regression ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) in <a href="#A2.F10" title="In B.2 Ornstein–Uhlenbeck Processes ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ B.2 Ornstein–Uhlenbeck Processes ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, OU processes exhibit different behavior compared to linear regression: the trade-off between in-distribution and out-of-distribution performance is less pronounced. As shown in both <a href="#A2.F10" title="In B.2 Ornstein–Uhlenbeck Processes ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ B.2 Ornstein–Uhlenbeck Processes ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, heavy-tailed priors maintain competitive in-distribution performance while still providing improved robustness to distribution shift.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F10.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_360000.png" id="A2.F10.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F10.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F10.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Student-t, <math id="A2.F10.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=3.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F10.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_420000.png" id="A2.F10.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F10.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F10.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Student-t, <math id="A2.F10.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=5.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=5.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F10.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_460000.png" id="A2.F10.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F10.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F10.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Student-t, <math id="A2.F10.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=10.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=10.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F10.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/x18.png" id="A2.F10.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F10.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A2.F10.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gaussian limit, <math id="A2.F10.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A2.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ornstein–Uhlenbeck processes with Student-<math id="A2.F10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> pretraining distributions: MSE as a function of ICL step for different task shift magnitudes. Unlike linear regression, heavy-tailed priors maintain strong in-distribution performance while providing superior robustness to perturbations. Baselines include predicting the last observed value and fitting an ARMA(5) model to the context.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F11" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F11.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_300000.png" id="A2.F11.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F11.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F11.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GenNormal, <math id="A2.F11.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F11.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_240000.png" id="A2.F11.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F11.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F11.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GenNormal, <math id="A2.F11.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.5</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F11.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/x19.png" id="A2.F11.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F11.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F11.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GenNormal, <math id="A2.F11.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F11.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_260000.png" id="A2.F11.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F11.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A2.F11.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GenNormal, <math id="A2.F11.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2.5</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><span id="A2.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ornstein–Uhlenbeck processes with generalized normal pretraining distributions (importance weighted): MSE as a function of ICL step for different task shift magnitudes. The shape parameter <math id="A2.F11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> shows consistent effects across perturbation levels, with all variants significantly outperforming simple baselines. Importance weighting provides modest improvements in robustness.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Volterra Processes</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present comprehensive results for stochastic Volterra equations (detailed in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Stochastic Volterra Equations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>), which model nonlinear processes with long-range dependencies and connections to fractional Brownian motion.
<a href="#A2.F12" title="In B.3 Volterra Processes ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> shows ICL error as a function of context length for different kernel exponents <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{1,1.5,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{1,1.5,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where smaller <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values correspond to stronger temporal dependencies.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results confirm our theoretical predictions from <a href="#S3" title="3 Theoretical framework ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>: as the kernel exponent <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> increases (weaker dependencies), both convergence speed and final performance improve significantly. This validates the dependency structure analysis in <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3.3 Generalization error ‣ 3 Theoretical framework ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A2.F13" title="In B.3 Volterra Processes ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> extends the generalization analysis from <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.3 Stochastic Volterra Equations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, demonstrating how the number of pretraining tasks <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> interacts with the temporal dependency parameter <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. The results show that processes with stronger dependencies (smaller <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) require substantially more training data to achieve comparable performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F12" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F12.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_100000.svg" id="A2.F12.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/270;" width="476" height="270"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F12.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F12.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Kernel exponent <math id="A2.F12.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F12.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_20000.svg" id="A2.F12.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/270;" width="476" height="270"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F12.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F12.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Kernel exponent <math id="A2.F12.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.5</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F12.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/icl_step_true_40000.svg" id="A2.F12.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/270;" width="476" height="270"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F12.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F12.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Kernel exponent <math id="A2.F12.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=2.0</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>: </span><span id="A2.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Stochastic Volterra equations: MSE as a function of ICL step across different kernel exponents <math id="A2.F12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Smaller <math id="A2.F12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values correspond to stronger long-range dependencies, leading to slower convergence and higher final error. The performance gap between different <math id="A2.F12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values demonstrates the impact of temporal dependency structure on ICL learning. Simple baselines provide reference points for comparison.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F13" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F13.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/x20.svg" id="A2.F13.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F13.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><math id="A2.F13.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=100</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F13.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/x21.svg" id="A2.F13.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F13.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><math id="A2.F13.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=500</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F13.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/x22.svg" id="A2.F13.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F13.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><math id="A2.F13.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1000</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F13.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01163v1/x23.svg" id="A2.F13.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F13.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><math id="A2.F13.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5000</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F13.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 13</span>: </span><span id="A2.F13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Generalization analysis for Volterra processes across different numbers of pretraining tasks <math id="A2.F13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Processes with stronger temporal dependencies (smaller <math id="A2.F13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) exhibit larger performance gaps at low <math id="A2.F13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3.3 Generalization error ‣ 3 Theoretical framework ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The dependency coefficients in our theory scale with <math id="A2.F13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, explaining why more training tasks are needed to achieve good performance for smaller <math id="A2.F13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We roughly follow the experimental setup used by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raventós et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
Our code is largely based on their implementation given in<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">‡</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">‡</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">‡</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          https://github.com/mansheej/icl-task-diversity
</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Data Generation</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In all experiments, task parameters <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled from the distribution mentioned in the main text, data sequences are sampled according to the task.
All task distributions during training are zero mean and unit variance in each dimension, except for the Volterra experiments where they are normalized to have standard deviation 0.2.
For testing, we sample <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mu\one,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝟙</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mu\one,I)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the shift value and <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\one" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝟙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\one</annotation></semantics></math> is the all ones vector, and the data is sampled according to this task.
Unless otherwise specified, a new set of tasks <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled for each training iteration. Otherwise, when the number of tasks is specified, we sample that many tasks at the start of training and use those same tasks throughout training.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Linear Regression</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a task parameter <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}}^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}}^{8}</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{8})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{8})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=\langle x_{i},\theta\rangle+\epsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=\langle x_{i},\theta\rangle+\epsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,0.5^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>0.5</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,0.5^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a context of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},y_{1}),\ldots,(x_{k},y_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},y_{1}),\ldots,(x_{k},y_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, the model is trained to predict <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the MSE loss.
At evaluation, we evaluate the model output against <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{\top}\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{\top}\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
We refer to the linear regression experiments in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raventós et al. (2023)</a></cite> for details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) Process</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The OU process is given by <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}X_{t}=\tau(\mu-X_{t})\mathrm{d}t+\sigma\mathrm{d}W_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}X_{t}=\tau(\mu-X_{t})\mathrm{d}t+\sigma\mathrm{d}W_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and has two parameters: <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.
We study a 8-dimensional process where <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{8}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma={0.5}I_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma={0.5}I_{8}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We consider the initial distribution of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{8})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{8}).</annotation></semantics></math>
Full paths of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled using the Euler-Maruyama method with a step size of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the sampling of tasks, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}}^{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}}^{9}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from the described distribution, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is then set to be the first 8 components of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3+0.2\times\sigma(-0.4\theta_{9})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3+0.2\times\sigma(-0.4\theta_{9})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function.
The model is trained to predict <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{(k+1)\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{(k+1)\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0},X_{\Delta t},\ldots,X_{k\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0},X_{\Delta t},\ldots,X_{k\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math> with the MSE loss with a maximum context length of 32.
For evaluation, we evaluate the model output against <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[X_{(k+1)\Delta t}|X_{0},X_{\Delta t},\ldots,X_{k\Delta t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[X_{(k+1)\Delta t}|X_{0},X_{\Delta t},\ldots,X_{k\Delta t}]</annotation></semantics></math> which is computable in closed form.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Volterra Process</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study a Volterra process in dimension 8
given by</p>
<table id="A3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=X_{0}+\int_{0}^{t}(t-s)^{-\alpha}b_{\theta}(X_{s})\mathrm{d}s+\int_{0}^{t}(t-s)^{-\alpha}\sigma\mathrm{d}W_{s}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=X_{0}+\int_{0}^{t}(t-s)^{-\alpha}b_{\theta}(X_{s})\mathrm{d}s+\int_{0}^{t}(t-s)^{-\alpha}\sigma\mathrm{d}W_{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the parameter <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen according to discrete values in <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,1.5,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,1.5,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.6I_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.6I_{8}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,I_{8})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,I_{8})</annotation></semantics></math> again.
<math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> a clipped two-layer neural network and hidden dimension 16: formally, with <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=(W_{1},b_{1},W_{2},b_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=(W_{1},b_{1},W_{2},b_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\theta}(x)=\mathrm{clip}(10(W_{2}\tanh(W_{1}x+b_{1})+b_{2}),-2,2)-0.1x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\theta}(x)=\mathrm{clip}(10(W_{2}\tanh(W_{1}x+b_{1})+b_{2}),-2,2)-0.1x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We subsample the paths <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{t})_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t})_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with step size <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=2</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain discrete samples <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(X_{0},X_{\Delta t},X_{2\Delta t},\ldots,)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{\Delta t},X_{2\Delta t},\ldots,)</annotation></semantics></math> and each
<math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed from past samples using 10 steps of the Euler-Maruyama method with step size <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t/10</annotation></semantics></math>.
The model is trained to predict <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{(k+1)\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{(k+1)\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0},X_{\Delta t},\ldots,X_{k\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0},X_{\Delta t},\ldots,X_{k\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math> with the MSE loss with a maximum context length of 32.
For evaluation, we evaluate the model output against <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[X_{(k+1)\Delta t}|X_{0},X_{\Delta t},\ldots,X_{k\Delta t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[X_{(k+1)\Delta t}|X_{0},X_{\Delta t},\ldots,X_{k\Delta t}]</annotation></semantics></math> which is computable in closed form.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Architecture and Optimization Details</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all experiments, we consider the architecture inspired by GPT-2 as used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raventós et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
For linear regression experiments, we use a context length of 64 points, 6 layers, embedding dimension of 32, 8 attention heads and an output dimension of 1.
For the other experiments, we use a context length of 32 points, 8 layers, embedding dimension of 128, 2 attention heads and an output dimension of 8.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All models were trained for <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations.
Experiments are run with AdamW optimizer with a weight decay of 0.1 with a cosine learning rate schedule and 50,000 warmup steps.
All experiments were run on NVIDIA H100 GPUs.
We performed a hyperparameter sweep over learning rate where we considered two learning rates and chose the best model.
Experiments are repeated 3 different times with different seeds.
LLMs were used to assist in code writing.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Task Selection</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we study how tasks are selected at test time in ICL.
This section is structured as follows.
First we consider an abstract setting for <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Preliminary Lemmas ‣ Appendix D Task Selection ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> and <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Template Task Selection Bound ‣ Appendix D Task Selection ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> where in <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Preliminary Lemmas ‣ Appendix D Task Selection ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> we state a few preliminary lemmas that will be useful in the analysis, and in <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Template Task Selection Bound ‣ Appendix D Task Selection ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> we prove a template task selection bound under minimal assumptions.
Then, in <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 ICL setting ‣ Appendix D Task Selection ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>, we reintroduce the ICL setting along with the detailed assumptions before proving the main task selection bound in <a href="#A4.SS4" title="D.4 Task Selection Bound for ICL ‣ Appendix D Task Selection ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a>, which is where the main contribution of this section lies.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Preliminary Lemmas</h3>

<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Kullback-Leibler divergence)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prob" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prob</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\probalt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\probalt</annotation></semantics></math> two probability measures on a measurable space <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence</em> from <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prob" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prob</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\probalt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\probalt</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="A4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathrm{KL}}\!(\prob\,\|\,\probalt)=\begin{cases}\int_{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}}\log\left(\frac{d\prob}{d\probalt}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})\right)d\prob({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})&amp;\text{if }\prob\ll\probalt\\
+\infty&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒳</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathrm{KL}}\!(\prob\,\|\,\probalt)=\begin{cases}\int_{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}}\log\left(\frac{d\prob}{d\probalt}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})\right)d\prob({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})&amp;\text{if }\prob\ll\probalt\\
+\infty&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now state the Donsker-Varadhan lemma, also known as the Gibbs variational principle.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma D.1</span></span><span id="A4.Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Donsker-Varadhan lemma, Gibbs variational principle)<span id="A4.Thmlemma1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="A4.Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prob" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prob</annotation></semantics></math> probability measure on a measurable <math id="A4.Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g}\colon{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}\to{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g}\colon{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}\to{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> a measurable function such that <math id="A4.Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ex_{\prob}[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g})]&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ex_{\prob}[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g})]&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have</span></p>
<table id="A4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log\ex_{\prob}[e^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})}]=\sup_{\probalt}\left\{\ex_{\probalt}[{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})]-{\mathrm{KL}}\!(\probalt\,\|\,\prob)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\ex_{\prob}[e^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})}]=\sup_{\probalt}\left\{\ex_{\probalt}[{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})]-{\mathrm{KL}}\!(\probalt\,\|\,\prob)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmlemma1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmlemma1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with equality attained in particular for <math id="A4.Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d\probalt}{d\prob}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})\propto e^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\probalt}{d\prob}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})\propto e^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}x})}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">See for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hellström et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rodríguez-Gálvez et al. (2024)</a></cite> for original references and proofs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let us state a technical consequence of this lemma that essentially corresponds to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang (2003, Lem. 3.1)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma D.2</span></span><span id="A4.Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}</annotation></semantics></math> a random variable on <math id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> distributed according to <math id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prob_{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prob_{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> a random variable on <math id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with prior distribution <math id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and with posterior distribution such that,
conditionally on <math id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\prob}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})=\frac{d\prob({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})}{d\prob({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\prob}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})=\frac{d\prob({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})}{d\prob({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="A4.Thmlemma2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}\colon{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}\times{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\Theta}\to{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}\colon{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}\times{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\Theta}\to{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathbb{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math> a measurable function.
Then,</span></p>
<table id="A4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ex_{#1}\left[{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]\right]\leq\ex_{#1}\left[{\mathrm{KL}}\!(\prob_{\!#1}(\cdot\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})\,\|\,{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi})\right]\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.220em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.387em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ex_{#1}\left[{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]\right]\leq\ex_{#1}\left[{\mathrm{KL}}\!(\prob_{\!#1}(\cdot\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})\,\|\,{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi})\right]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We apply <a href="#A4.Thmlemma1" title="Lemma D.1 (Donsker-Varadhan lemma, Gibbs variational principle). ‣ D.1 Preliminary Lemmas ‣ Appendix D Task Selection ‣ How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning?Task Selection, Generalization, and Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> with <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})={\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}g}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})={\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]</annotation></semantics></math> conditionally on <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain</span></p>
<table id="A4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ex_{#1}\left[{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]-{\mathrm{KL}}\!(\prob_{\!#1}(\cdot\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})\,\|\,{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.220em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.387em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ex_{#1}\left[{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]-{\mathrm{KL}}\!(\prob_{\!#1}(\cdot\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})\,\|\,{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]\right)\right]\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]\right)\right]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">We then have</span></p>
<table id="A4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ex_{#1}\left[\exp\ex_{#1}\left[{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]-{\mathrm{KL}}\!(\prob_{\!#1}(\cdot\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})\,\|\,{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi})\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>exp</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.220em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.387em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ex_{#1}\left[\exp\ex_{#1}\left[{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]-{\mathrm{KL}}\!(\prob_{\!#1}(\cdot\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})\,\|\,{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi})\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\ex_{#1}\left[\exp\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]\right)\right]=1\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\ex_{#1}\left[\exp\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\exp({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}L}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}))\right]\right)\right]=1\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">and the result follows by Jensen’s inequality with the convex function <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Template Task Selection Bound</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us start with a template task selection bound under minimal assumptions. This proof is adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang (2003, Thm. 4.1)</a></cite> to the case of non-i.i.d. data and when the true task is not necessarily in the support of the prior.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A4.Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition D.1</span></span><span id="A4.Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Template task selection bound)<span id="A4.Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}</annotation></semantics></math> a random variable on <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> distributed according to <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prob_{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prob_{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> a random variable on <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with prior distribution <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> such that,
conditionally on <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is distributed according to</span></p>
<table id="A4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\prob}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})=\frac{d\prob({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})}{d\prob({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\prob}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})=\frac{d\prob({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X}\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})}{d\prob({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}X})}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\pi}(d{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(D.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, we have, for any <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}_{0}}\in{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta}_{0}}\in{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math>,
for any <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\rho}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\rho}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.Thmproposition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\alpha}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\alpha}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ex_{#1}\left[-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\left(\frac{d\prob_{\!#1}(\cdot\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})}{d\prob_{\!#1}(\cdot)}\right)^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\rho}}\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.220em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.387em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ex_{#1}\left[-\log\ex_{#1}\left[\left(\frac{d\prob_{\!#1}(\cdot\nonscript\&gt;|\nonscript\&gt;\mathopen{}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\theta})}{d\prob_{\!#1}(\cdot)}\right)^{{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}\rho}}\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
